<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">François Coillard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1904">1904</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">François Coillard was a French Protestant missionary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Evangelical Missionary Society. After two decades in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a> he travelled north and in 1886 began a mission among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Lozi</a> of Barotseland, founding stations at Sefula and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lealui">Lealui</a>. He became an adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewanika">Lewanika</a> and encouraged him to seek British protection, a decision that led to the concessions of 1890.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francois-coillard.jpg" alt="François Coillard" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fran%C3%A7ois_Coillard01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Coillard was born on 17 July 1834 at Asnières-les-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourges">Bourges</a> in the Cher, the youngest of the seven children of François Coillard and his wife Madeleine, both of Huguenot descent. His father died in 1836 and left the family nearly destitute. He entered the Protestant School at Asnières at 15 and later studied at Strasbourg University, offering himself to the Paris Evangelical Missionary Society in 1854.</p><p>He trained under Eugène Casalis, a veteran of the southern African mission, and was ordained at the Oratoire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1857. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> on 6 November 1857, on the eve of a war between Basutoland and the Orange Free State in which the French stations were destroyed, and his first task was to open a new station at Leribe. On 26 February 1861 he married Christina Mackintosh, daughter of a Scottish Baptist minister, who shared his travels as a co-worker; they had no children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the disputes of 1865 Coillard helped negotiations between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basuto</a> chiefs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophilus-shepstone">Theophilus Shepstone</a> of Natal. Boer forces from the Orange Free State forced the evacuation of Leribe in April 1866, and he worked in Natal until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over Basutoland in 1868. Encouraged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-moffat">Robert Moffat</a> of Kuruman, he led a party with four Basuto evangelists north, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> in May 1877, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> chiefs refused them and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobengula">Lobengula</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> forbade them to preach. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khama-iii">Khama III</a> of the Tswana then suggested Barotseland.</p><p>Coillard's expedition of 1884 found Barotseland in turmoil, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewanika">Lewanika</a> exiled and a usurper on the throne. His early dealings with the usurper cost him credit when Lewanika returned, and he was received at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lealui">Lealui</a> only in March 1886. Lewanika and Coillard misunderstood the ties between the British crown and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lochner-concession">Lochner Concession</a> of 27 June 1890 assigned the kingdom to the company, which for seven years paid none of its promised £2000 a year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Christina Coillard died on 28 October 1891, and the mission suffered high mortality from tropical fevers. After a serious illness in 1895 Coillard spent the period 1896–8 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1903 a breakaway movement of his converts led by Willie Mokalapa shook him further. He died of haematuric fever at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lealui">Lealui</a> on 27 May 1904 and was buried near his wife at Sefula. His letters, published in 1889 as Sur le Haut-Zambèze and in English in 1897, made him a hero to mission supporters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “François Coillard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Barotseland is one of the best-documented kingdoms of south-central Africa, thanks to oral traditions recorded in the colonial period, the writings of David Livingstone, who stayed with the Kololo in the 1850s, the French Protestant missionary <b>François Coillard</b>, who worked in the kingdom from 1886, and twentieth-century anthropologists.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Asnières-lès-Bourges, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lealui">Lealui</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.12,2.41&amp;year=1879" aria-label="Asnières-lès-Bourges on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4606 510 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of François Coillard"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5056 780h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5056 780h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.12,2.41&amp;year=1879">What was here in 1879? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
